# EVT Psychology Reference

## Expectancy Violations Theory

Judee Burgoon's theory explains how people notice and evaluate deviations from expected behavior. A violation is not automatically persuasive: its effect depends on the communicator's perceived reward value and whether the deviation is evaluated positively or negatively.

**Writing consequence:** identify whose expectation is being violated, make the deviation legible, and provide enough credibility or evidence for the audience to treat it as relevant rather than random.

Source: [Wiley reference](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/9781118540190.wbeic102).

## Prediction Error and Attention

Learning research treats prediction error as the difference between expected and received events. Mismatch can select attention and update a mental model; surprise alone does not determine what is learned.

**Writing consequence:** use the violation to open a model-update opportunity. The next beat must explain what changed and why it matters. Do not confuse arousal with understanding.

Sources: [Holland, 2016](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4862921/); [Sinclair et al.](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2117625118).

## Information-Gap Theory

George Loewenstein's account says curiosity arises when people notice a gap between what they know and what they want to know. The gap must be salient and connected to something that matters.

**Writing consequence:** reveal enough context to make the gap felt, then delay only the explanation that closes it. A gap with no payoff becomes frustration.

Source: [Loewenstein, 1994](https://www.cmu.edu/dietrich/sds/docs/loewenstein/PsychofCuriosity.pdf).

## Cognitive Dissonance

Festinger's theory explains pressure to reduce inconsistency between beliefs, actions, and new information. Directly humiliating the audience often triggers defensive rejection.

**Writing consequence:** preserve one part of the audience's identity as an anchor, challenge one specific assumption, and offer a face-saving better model.

Source: [Cooper, 2019](https://rips-irsp.com/articles/10.5334/irsp.277).

## Uses and Gratifications

Uses-and-gratifications research treats audiences as active users who select media to satisfy information, identity, emotional release, social connection, and entertainment needs.

**Writing consequence:** define the desired takeaway before designing the violation. The same fact needs different openings for an audience seeking orientation, recognition, status, agency, relief, or wonder.

Source: [Falgoust et al., 2022](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2772501422000112).

## Three-Second Heuristic

The three-second rule is a short-video production heuristic, not a universal scientific constant. The research above supports attention to mismatch and curiosity, but does not prove one retention threshold for every platform.

Use it as an editorial gate: the first beat should expose topic plus deviation. Validate it with actual retention data.

## Desire Model

| Desire | Viewer wants to leave with | Useful violation |
|---|---|---|
| Orientation | a clearer map | the obvious explanation is incomplete |
| Recognition | a feeling put into words | the “normal” person has the hidden problem |
| Status | a shareable insight | insiders are missing the real mechanism |
| Agency | a decision rule | the common action produces the opposite result |
| Relief | less uncertainty or shame | the apparent failure has another cause |
| Wonder | a larger possibility | an ordinary detail reveals a vast system |

Choose one primary desire. Multiple desires may coexist, but one must govern the payoff.
